```java
interface Dog { default String speak() { return "Woof"; } }
interface RoboAnimal { default String speak() { return "Beep"; } }

// Compile error: RoboDog inherits two conflicting default speak() methods
class RoboDog implements Dog, RoboAnimal {
    // Must resolve explicitly:
    public String speak() { return Dog.super.speak(); }
}
```

Java can't have this ambiguity through classes (only one `extends`), but it reappears through interfaces once both provide a `default` body. The fix is explicit disambiguation — `Dog.super.speak()` — the language forces you to pick.

```java
interface Dog { default String speak() { return "Woof"; } }
interface RoboAnimal { default String speak() { return "Beep"; } }

class RoboDog implements Dog, RoboAnimal {
    // no override at all
}
// error: class RoboDog inherits unrelated defaults for speak() from
// types Dog and RoboAnimal — RoboDog must override speak()
```

Java refuses to guess. Rather than silently picking one interface's default (which would be a hidden, order-dependent decision), the compiler forces `RoboDog` to override `speak()` explicitly — the ambiguity is a compile-time error, never a runtime surprise.
